  "strategy": "[Section 1: Approach and Rationale]\n\nWe propose a fundamentally different approach for solving the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. Rather than relying on traditional optimization heuristics and multi-stage procedures, we leverage mathematical optimization techniques and a novel representation. The key insight is that the minimum overlap problem can be reinterpreted as an optimal transport problem between two distributions on the circle, which allows us to exploit continuous relaxations and geometry-aware optimization.\n\nOur approach treats the overlap minimization as a two-point correlation problem on the torus. We introduce a low-dimensional coordinate system based on spherical harmonics, which captures the translation invariance property central to the problem. This harmonic representation provides both convexity guarantees for certain formulations and natural regularization through smoothness constraints.\n\nThe central innovation is a transformation from the time/space domain to harmonic frequencies, decomposing the optimization into independent mode coefficients. Each mode corresponds to a particular frequency component. This transformation not only reduces the effective dimensionality but also provides clear geometric interpretations for the optimization variables.\n\nWe formulate the constrained optimization as a Nonlinear Semidefinite Program (NLSDP) on the Stiefel manifold, allowing us to maintain probabilistic consistency and ensure marginal constraints. The solution procedure involves projecting distributional representations onto the simplex and applying Riemannian gradient descent with exponential integrators to handle the high-curvature geometric constraints efficiently.\n\n[Section 2: Mathematical Specification](2.1) Representation of Objective\n\nWe define probability distribution p on the circle T = \u211d/\u2124 (normalized by dx=2/n_points). The overlap integral becomes the integral of p(x)p(y+\u03c4) dx dy dy/dx (in the limit n\u2192\u221e), more naturally expressed as:\n\n   C = \u222b\u2080\u00b2 dx\u2081 \u222b\u2080\u00b2 dx\u2082 p(x\u2081) p(x\u2082) \u03b4(x\u2081 - x\u2082 - k) / (dx dx)   [continuous limit]\n\nEquivalently:\n\n   C = (2/n) \u2207ll_y p \u22c5 p (k) - some constant\n\n(2.2) Optimization Variables and Constraints\n\nLet p be represented as a Fourier series:\n\n   p(\u03b8) = \u2211_{m=-M}^{M} a_m e^{2\u03c0i m \u03b8}\n\nWhere \u03b8 is the circle parameterization. The integral constraint translates to:\n\n   \u2211_{m} a_0 = n_points/2 / (n_points/2 for dx=2) wait no...\n\nIn our discrete setting:\n\n   \u222b\u2080\u00b2 p(x) dx = 1\n\nWith p discrete, this becomes sum(h_i)*dx = 1.\n\nThe correlation variables are transformed into:\n\n   c_k = \u222b p(x)p(x+k) dx = \u2211_{m} a_m\u00b2 + 2\u2211_{m<n} Re[a_m conj(a_n)] (if exact) but for large n approximately.\n\n(2.3) Objective Formulation\n\nWe minimize:\n\n   minimize_Cmax = max_k c_k\n\nsubject to:\n\n   \u222b p(x) dx = 1\n   0 \u2264 p(x) \u2264 1\n\nbecomes in Fourier space:\n\n   \u2211_{m} a_m = constraint for the integral\n   |a_m| \u2264 some bound for the probability amplitude\n\nBut note: we must use constraint forms that are compatible with convex optimization.\n\n(2.4) Solving Approach\n\nWe solve the following optimization problem:\n\n   minimize_C_max\n   subject to\n     c_k \u2264 C_max, for all k\n     \u222b p = 1\n     0 \u2264 p \u2264 1\n\nIn terms of modes, these become:\n\n     \u2211_{m} a_m e^{i\u00b7m\u00b7k} \u2264 C_max, for all k\n     \u2211_{m} a_m = target\n     |a_m| \u2264 1/2 (approximately, since we're on a circle of circumference 1)\n\n(2.5) Solving Formulation Through Riemannian Projections\n\nWe employ a lifted formulation with the marginal condition encoded using an augmented Lagrangian method. The active manifold structure allows for automatic satisfaction of constraints through projection. These projections are implemented as geodesic flows on the Stiefel manifold.\n\n[Section 3: Implementation Procedure](3.1) Step-by-Step Execution\n\nOur implementation will proceed through the following stages:\n\n**Stage 0: Initial Setup & Heuristic Initialization**\n\n   1. Input: random seed, time budget, optional parent solution\n   2. If parent available: `p = downsample_parent(initial_h_values)`\n      Else: heuristic construction using Nyquist sampling\n   3. Harmonic coefficients: p\u03b8 \u2192 am using FFT\n   4. Set up Augmented Lagrangian framework for C_max and marginal constraints\n   5. Kernel matrix K for all lags (computed once)\n\n**Stage 1: Sparse Fourier Initialization**\n\n   1. Identify sparse representation using thresholded Singular Value Decomposition\n   2. Construct C_max_minimizer by approximately solving the TV-like relaxation\n   3. Set initial target value `C_target`\n\n**Stage 2: Riemannian Optimization**\n\n   1. Initialize Stiefel manifold elements from Stage 1 solution\n   2. Use Riemannian gradient descent with exponential integrator on the manifold\n            Option: Use either high-dimensional gradient or manifold projection\n\n**Stage 3: Multi-Scale Convexification**\n\n   1. Employ convexification for high-C_k modes through ball-averaging\n   2. Coarsen the grid and interpolate solution to fill gaps between scales\n   3. Incrementally reduce smoothing (i.e., increase M_max)\n\n**Stage 4: Iterative Refinement**\n\n   1. Implement any of several smoothing/denoising strategies:\n        SSVT (singular value thresholding)\n        ISTA (Iterative Shrinkage-Thresholding Algorithm)\n        ADMM (Alternating Direction Method of Multipliers)\n   2. Monitor two metrics:\n        - Rate of constraint violation\n        - Ratio of curvatures between manifold and dual space\n   3. Periodically rebalance between manifold and Riemannian components as these metrics change\n\n(3.2) Helper algorithmic components\n\n   1. **Manifold projection (`proj_stiefel`)**\n   2. **Geodesic distance (`geodesic`)**\n   3. **Riemannian gradient (`riemannian_grad`)**\n   4. **Convex hull repair (`convex_hull_proj`)**\n   5. **Augmented Lagrangian solver (`aug_lag_slsqp`)**\n   6. **Spherical harmonic transform (`fourier_transform_sphere`)**\n\n(3.3) Geometric Condition and Repair\n\n   The solution must satisfy:\n\n        a) All probability modes are complex numbers with |a_m| \u2264 1 (for normalized circle)\n        b) The marginal constraint: sum_m a_m = n/2 * dx\n        c) The overlap constraints: |c_k - C_max| \u2264 0 \u2200k\n\n   Implement repair operators:\n\n        - If mode violates |a_m|\u22641, project onto unit disk\n        - If integral constraint violated, normalize the real-part sum\n        - If any c_k exceeds current C_max, augment the constraint set\n\n[Section 4: Search Choices and Variations](4.1) Configurable Algorithm Variants\n\nWe implement seven design variations to systematically explore the parameter space:\n\n1. **Fourier Basis Scaling (\u03b1)**: Controls the sparsity of the representation, with valid range [0, log(n_points)]\n   - Default: \u03b1 = 0.5 * math.log(n_points)\n\n2. **Manifold Penalty (\u03b2)**: Regularization weight for manifold projection, range [0, \u221e)\n   - Default: \u03b2 = 0.05\n\n3. **Augmented Lagrangian Envelope (\u03b3)**: Penalty parameter for interval [5, 10]\n   - Default: \u03b3 = 3\n\n4. **Probability Distribution Type (\u03b4)**: Mixed probability type, enum{gaussian, uniform, exponential}\n   - Default: exponential\n\n5. **Initial Heuristic (\u03b7)**: Selection between three initial heuristics\n   - Default: 'mid' (midpoint-based)\n\n6. **Sampling Strategy (\u03be)**: Clustering method, enum{sobol, pseudorandom, t_distribution}\n   - Default: sobol\n\n7. **Dimensionality (\u03c1)**: Number of Fourier components, range [1, min(100, n_points/2)]\n   - Default: \u03c1 = 50\n\n[Section 5: Budget and Robustness](5.1) Resource Allocation\n\nWe allocate 1100s according to this scheme:\n\n   1. Initialization & Preprocessing (5%)\n   2. Sparse Fourier Transform (15%)\n   3. Riemannian Optimization (40%)\n   4. Multi-Scale Processing (20%)\n   5. Iterative Refinement (15%)\n   6. Validation & Repair (5%)\n\nWe use the following triggers for adaptive budgeting:\n\n   - Lowest-C_max detected: boost refinement in that direction\n   - Stagnation detection (slope analysis on C_max): increase problem dimension\n   - Failure awareness (how many NaN/inf before recovery): fallback to parent\n\n[Section 6: Validation and Contract](6.1) Output Validation\n\nAll produced solutions h must satisfy:\n\n   1. \\frac{0}{n}\n(6.2) End-to-End Validation Function\n\n```python\ndef validate_solution(h, n_points):\n    dx = 2.0 / n_points\n    h_sum = np.sum(h) * dx\n    # Check integral\n    if h_sum < 0.9999 or h_sum > 1.0001:\n        return False, \"Integral constraint failed: {:.6f}\".format(h_sum)\n    # Check bounds\n    if np.min(h) < 0 or np.max(h) > 1:\n        return False, \"Bounds violation\"\n    # Check overlap\n    try:\n        c = np.max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')) * dx\n        is_valid = c < 0.38085\n        return is_valid, \"Overlap: {:.6f}\".format(c)\n    except Exception as e:\n        return False, \"Error in validation: {}\".format(e)\n```\n\n(6.3) Final Representation and Output Format\n\nOur final output will be a structure containing:\n\n   - h_values: list of {0,1} values (binarized)\n   - C5_bound: the validated minimum overlap\n   - n_points: 560 fixed by parent\n\nBut note: the algorithm is designed to work at any n_points that is a multiple of 16 for the spherical harmonic transform.",
